WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.770
There seems to be a bit of a tradition

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.540
of making a special video when your

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.160
YouTube channel passes a million

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.049
subscribers.
'It is weighty...'

00:00:09.049 --> 00:00:10.349
Now I was a

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:13.469
bit slow making mine 
because - well - you're

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:16.289
about to see why. Essentially I wanted to

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:19.350
show how big a million is; a way to kind

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.660
of visualise it. This is where the idea

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.300
of printing pi to a million digits came

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.609
from. Of course a million digits of pi

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:28.949
have been known for a long time, you can

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:30.869
see them on the internet, even buy a book

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:32.940
of them; but I wanted to print it like a

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.910
conventional number, left to right on a

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.030
single piece of paper. I had no idea what

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.770
this would look like or even how big it

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.260
would need to be; so after a few emails

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.170
and phone calls - and this is cutting a

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.660
long story short - I ended up travelling

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.969
to Denmark to a printing company called

00:00:50.969 --> 00:00:55.050
HSA Systems. These people were amazing

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.000
and it seems no job was too big for them.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.879
Here I am with a guy called Torben who

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:02.070
was an absolute star and is the guy who

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.080
really made this happen from a technical

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.409
standpoint. For various reasons, including

00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:09.689
the fact it's a monospace font, we

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:12.900
decided to print pi in Courier New. The

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.110
point size we chose was 8. This means

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.409
each digit was 40 pixels wide, one pixel

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:22.049
is one six hundredth of an inch, so each

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:24.540
digit was a fifteenth of an inch across.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.970
Now this meant our million decimal

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.939
places, or a million and two if you count

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:32.790
the three and the decimal point, would be

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:35.750
1.05 miles long, nearly

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.180
1,700 metres - how is that for some

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.579
serious Numberphile action? Here we are

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:44.430
doing a test print run, and as you can

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.410
see it's on white paper.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:51.420
Now Numberphile fans will know that we

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.910
love brown paper, and two reels of

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.640
special brown paper had been donated by

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.590
a UK company called Millingtons;

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:01.890
but it had not arrived. It was due to

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.440
arrive, it just hadn't. So it looked like

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.540
we might resort to using the white paper,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.580
which was easier to get our hands on, it

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.430
was kind of like the backup. But, at the

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.370
last minute, like the last minute, the

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:18.920
brown paper arrived.
- 'You're our hero!'

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:26.250
There we go, here it is being mounted and

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.590
we're about to start printing. By the way

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.800
this guy is Hugh McPartlan, he's HSA's man

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.840
in the UK, he's with me here in Denmark,

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.340
and he's the real reason the whole

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.350
project really succeeded, you'll see more

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.180
of him later.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.530
I have to say it was amazing how

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.900
smoothly the whole thing went and a

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.119
print run took about 
48 and a half minutes.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:00.980
It turns out the whole thing hinged on

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.800
this wheel, the encoder, which measures

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.470
how far along the paper we are. Now it's

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.150
just three one hundredths of a

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.310
millimeter off being a perfect circle

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.980
which means, I'm told, our spacing was

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.140
pretty good. And I must say I do find it

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.390
lovely how circles, and of course pi,

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:20.270
were used and were so instrumental in

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.460
our printing of pi. Now in addition to

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.830
just the numbers, the decimal places of
pi,

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.110
we included markers all the way along to

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.240
count the decimal places. There was a

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.690
marker every 10 digits. This was so we'd

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.180
be able to find parts of pi more easily

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.600
when the time came. 
- Okay so I'm looking for

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.140
216182...'
- We also had a marker every

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.390
yard along the paper to give us an idea

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:46.210
of distance travelled.

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:53.299
Now I would have preferred metres, being

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:55.459
bit of a metric man myself, but for

00:03:55.459 --> 00:03:57.439
complicated reasons it was hard to make

00:03:57.439 --> 00:03:59.689
this synchronise. It all relates to the

00:03:59.689 --> 00:04:01.400
fact the technology we were using to

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:05.120
print with is in dots per inch. It made

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.489
our chunks of pi more easily divided

00:04:07.489 --> 00:04:09.349
into imperial units, we had a lot of

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:10.609
problems when we tried to do it in

00:04:10.609 --> 00:04:12.650
metres. Now here's a quick interview with

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.530
Jesper, he's the boss at HSA, and another

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:18.169
man we owe a huge debt to.
- Well we do not

00:04:18.169 --> 00:04:22.039
only do one type of printer, right? We do

00:04:22.039 --> 00:04:24.440
a lot of different printers and printer

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.169
controllers that were manufactured from
HP

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:29.030
technology but also Xaar technology out of

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.520
England; Cambridge, England. We do both

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.259
software, we do the printed circuit

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:35.449
boards, we do the 
printheads, the controllers,

00:04:35.449 --> 00:04:37.370
everything right? We do a lot of

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.380
number printing. And that could be, for

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:41.539
example, lottery; it could be pin numbers;

00:04:41.539 --> 00:04:43.910
it could be a unique identifier for

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.789
products. For example within the the food

00:04:46.789 --> 00:04:49.220
industry, right, well they would- may

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.039
require a unique identification of a

00:04:52.039 --> 00:04:54.620
certain product. And we would either

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:57.289
generate the number from- straight from

00:04:57.289 --> 00:04:59.630
our software or we would load a database

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:01.909
and then print the numbers from the

00:05:01.909 --> 00:05:03.979
database. Well numbers, mathematics, is

00:05:03.979 --> 00:05:07.090
very much a big part of our day right?

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.740
Calculating DPIss and pulses per

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.500
rotation and what have we not so- and

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:14.210
I'll say we're not the ones doing the

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.909
hard mathematics, that's the the guys in

00:05:16.909 --> 00:05:19.520
Copenhagen who who do the software and

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.699
also the core- the core of the software

00:05:22.699 --> 00:05:24.590
doing the hard mathematics.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.349
Namely calculating how to get a drop out

00:05:27.349 --> 00:05:29.539
of the printhead at the right time, right

00:05:29.539 --> 00:05:32.120
size, etc etc. So- but yes

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.190
mathematics is a very big part of our

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:36.830
our working day that's for sure.
- (Brady: Tell me)

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:38.000
what you know about pi?

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.627
What I know about pi? Okay. Well-
- (It's not a maths test!)

00:05:41.627 --> 00:05:42.950
There's not a math test

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:46.780
(You obviously know the number?)
- Do I have to wear

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.240
flip-flops and a white sheet now or what?

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.500
(But like, you've never 
print- have you ever)

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.409
(had to print out pi before?)
- No, never no,

00:05:54.409 --> 00:05:57.229
We've never done that. We do- we do

00:05:57.229 --> 00:05:58.820
make use of pi of course in our

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:00.529
everyday work, that's for sure, and we

00:06:00.529 --> 00:06:02.300
know a bit about pi because we have to

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.490
do a bit of calculations when it comes
to

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:07.469
our tooling machines etc. 
- (You've got circles everywhere!)

00:06:07.469 --> 00:06:09.289
We've got circles everywhere, 
yeah, and of course we

00:06:09.289 --> 00:06:10.880
use pi a lot but we have never

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:14.960
done 1 million digits pi before on a

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.039
printing machine, so that's quite

00:06:16.039 --> 00:06:17.960
exciting.
- (Have you done any number to)

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.900
(a million digits or am I your first?)

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:21.979
Well, you're the first where we've done a

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:23.930
million digits that's for sure.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:27.059
(There you go Torben, you're 
holding a big piece of pi there!)

00:06:28.489 --> 00:06:32.750
So after printing our reel, what next?

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.599
Well, wrap it up of course, and then print

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:45.819
another one! We figured this would be a

00:06:45.819 --> 00:06:48.370
handy backup if something went wrong. If

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.199
you're interested, the second roll was

00:06:50.199 --> 00:06:52.270
never actually used and it's still in my

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:55.120
office. If you're some wealthy math

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:56.860
enthusiast you can even make me an offer

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.620
if you like - I'll warn you,
postage will not

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:02.039
be cheap. Now I also still have the HP

00:07:02.039 --> 00:07:04.389
cartridges we used to print the whole

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:08.050
thing. Amazingly we used only 20% of a

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.210
cartridge, just over eight milliliters of

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.069
ink. This is what eight milliliters of

00:07:12.069 --> 00:07:15.520
ink looks like, or 194 million tiny drops

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:17.620
from the printhead. The people at HP are

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:19.479
rightly proud of what their stuff can

00:07:19.479 --> 00:07:21.909
do, it's called thermal inkjet technology.

00:07:21.909 --> 00:07:23.889
They were also super excited when they

00:07:23.889 --> 00:07:26.050
found out about the pi project and later

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:27.460
they helped me cover some of the

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:28.960
production costs. This was really

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.580
generous of them and I'm very grateful.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:33.610
So, anyway, two rolls were printed and

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.099
then it was back to the UK for me and

00:07:36.099 --> 00:07:38.080
the rolls were posted not far behind.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.000
Here they are when they arrived at my

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.699
house. Next was arranging a way to unroll

00:07:42.699 --> 00:07:44.560
the paper. Now after a wild goose chase,

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.930
and calling several Air Force bases, I

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.750
was able to hire the former RAF base at

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:52.150
Bruntingthorpe - that's
in Leicestershire in

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:54.520
England - which is now used as a car

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:56.979
testing track and aviation museum, they've

00:07:56.979 --> 00:07:58.990
got all these Cold War jets there. I have

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:01.449
to say, if you like planes or exotic

00:08:01.449 --> 00:08:03.969
sports cars, this is a place that will

00:08:03.969 --> 00:08:06.250
blow your mind. Now a section of their

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.710
two-mile runway was sectioned off so we

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:10.630
could do our thing; it turns out no one

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:12.190
else was using the facility that day we

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:14.560
had the whole runway to ourselves. But we

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.000
were there with no real sense of how

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.580
difficult this might be, no idea how a

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:20.710
mile of paper could be effectively

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:23.590
unrolled. But that man Hugh, together with

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:26.050
a chap named Jon Kenny, devised an

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:28.029
ingenious contraption. Here are the

00:08:28.029 --> 00:08:30.279
designs they made, and here is the real

00:08:30.279 --> 00:08:33.249
thing after it was made by Jon.

00:08:59.199 --> 00:09:01.509
Now I've got to admit I had a mild heart

00:09:01.509 --> 00:09:04.103
attack here thinking the
paper wouldn't fit

00:09:10.943 --> 00:09:14.410
Hmmm, not sure about that. But it got on

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:17.649
and we were able to start. Numberphile

00:09:17.649 --> 00:09:20.529
regular and pi enthusiast Matt Parker

00:09:20.529 --> 00:09:23.500
came along to be our tour guide; I should

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.149
probably thank him for giving up a whole

00:09:25.149 --> 00:09:27.550
day but I think he enjoyed himself far

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:32.920
too much as it is.
- This is very exciting.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.389
Now the unrolling was okay, and even

00:09:40.389 --> 00:09:42.579
the rain was tolerable though a nuisance,

00:09:42.579 --> 00:09:45.269
but the biggest problem was wind.

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:14.079
The wind was mercifully very light

00:10:14.079 --> 00:10:16.269
this day but it still played havoc with

00:10:16.269 --> 00:10:18.310
the paper. Luckily I'd brought some bags

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:20.079
of sand; we were able to use this to

00:10:20.079 --> 00:10:21.920
weigh down the paper.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:38.470
But the sand was

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:41.230
running out fast. We sent for more cones

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:42.670
which was stored elsewhere on the site,

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:44.030
they helped,

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:51.900
and then I went for a drive to the end

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:53.400
of the runway and found something else

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:56.910
that helped, these cone bases which were

00:10:56.910 --> 00:10:58.920
nice and heavy but not too tall so we

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.090
could stack them in the back of the car.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.690
Now while the runway crew worked really

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:12.030
hard on the rollout, Matt and I spent

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.190
some time further back recording pi

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:17.130
facts. Now much of this was cut from the

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:19.200
main video, that's just how things go, we

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.300
needed to keep the video short, but now

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:23.730
I'm going to show you more of them. Enjoy

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.170
these and when they're finished I'll

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:27.620
tell you more about how the day ended.

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:29.430
Pi - what a number. Abs-

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:31.860
it's unparalleled in mathematics. And, as

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:33.240
we all know, the digits never end; they

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:34.920
just keep on going forever and ever

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:37.350
which is incredible. And you can download

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:38.970
huge numbers of these digits and some

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:41.940
people, insanely, will print off tens

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:43.260
of thousands of them and make like a

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:46.320
poster. Not Brady, no. At Numberphile he

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:50.340
has printed off a million digits. So this

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:53.250
is the beginning and this is a mile; so

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:54.930
you can see they're unraveling it

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:57.720
further up there. there is one mile of a

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.450
continuous piece of paper- 
Alright so it

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.400
is raining a little bit, but that's fine,

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:04.260
that's the great thing about pi, it
doesn't

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:06.390
change if it gets wet. So we're going to

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:09.600
begin our exploration and-
- (Wow, pi is the same when it's raining?)

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:12.660
I know, who knew! It's moisture invariant.
So here

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:15.810
I've marked the first 10 decimal places

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.520
that only use three different digits.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:23.100
Okay here we've got 1565

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:26.766
51565666111;

00:12:26.766 --> 00:12:30.060
and that is a run of 1,2,3,4,5,6,

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:33.060
7,8,9,10,11,12,13,14 - 15! -

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:35.220
which only uses three different digits,

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:37.890
so there you go. I mean-
so here at Numberphile

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:40.170
we're answering all the questions that

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:41.540
no one's ever asked.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:44.580
Ok so we've come a fair way, where are we

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:48.380
now? We're up to a 121

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:52.410
yards, so we're at 11 squared yards, and

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.510
we're working away along. And I mean

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:58.230
famously people try and memorise pi. And

00:12:58.230 --> 00:12:59.790
so I've got it down to, you know, the

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:02.520
first couple of digits 
and - oh, one of our

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.200
support vehicles is coming up Brady, you

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:06.570
might want to pop out of the way, no it's

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:10.560
not. He stopped, he's getting out.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:16.220
89,000-

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:20.690
If you take the alphabet; so A becomes 1,

00:13:20.690 --> 00:13:23.390
B becomes 2, C becomes 3 and so on

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:25.850
you can turn words into letters. So my

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:29.900
name is Matt, MATT; M is the 13th letter

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:31.490
of the alphabet, A is the first, T is the

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.255
twentieth - so I'll be looking for

00:13:33.255 --> 00:13:38.780
13012020 for 13 1 20 20; and you 
can- oh we've gone

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:40.090
past it.

00:13:40.090 --> 00:13:45.920
So that 02, that
is a - I'll put up here - that

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:50.150
is a B; work my way backwards. So before

00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:54.770
that is a 12, that is a L, before that is

00:13:54.770 --> 00:14:00.040
an 8 that's an H, before that is a 15

